{0 Type-safe router}

The generated [Router] module is the application API. Application components
should not import [RouterRuntime], [RouterReact], or [RouterServer] directly.

{1 Declaring routes}

Routes live in one manifest file compiled by [server-reason-react.router-gen].
The manifest is a single [Router.make] expression:

{[
Router.make(
  ~basePath="/demo/router",
  ~search={ text: Router.Search.optional(string) },
  ~layout=Pages.AppLayout,
  ~loading=Pages.GlobalLoading,
  ~error=Pages.AppError,
  ~notFound=Pages.NotFound,
  [
    Router.route(Home, ~page=Pages.App, ~path="/"),
    Router.route(NewNote, ~page=Pages.NewNote, ~path="/new", ~loading=Pages.NewNoteLoading),
    Router.group(
      ~path="/:id<NoteId.t>",
      ~layout=Pages.NoteLayout,
      ~loader=Pages.NoteLoader,
      ~loaderAs_=note,
      [
        Router.route(Note, ~page=Pages.Note, ~path="/", ~loading=Pages.NoteLoading),
        Router.route(EditNote, ~page=Pages.EditNote, ~path="/edit", ~loading=Pages.EditNoteLoading),
      ],
    ),
  ],
);
]}

[Router.route] declares a leaf page with a variant name. [Router.group] nests
routes under a shared path prefix and layout. Both accept attachments, as does
the root.

Attachments take modules, and the generator resolves the convention inside
each module:

- [~page], [~layout], [~loading], [~notFound], [~invalidSearch] resolve
  [Module.make], a component.
- [~loader] resolves [Module.load]. The scope's components receive the loader
  result under the module name ([Pages.NoteLoader] becomes [~noteLoader]), or
  under the [~loaderAs_] name when given.
- [~error] at the root resolves an error policy: a module with a type [t] (the
  application error), [status: t => Router.Status.t], and a [make] component.
  On groups and routes, [~error] is a boundary component.
- [~metadata] and [~headers] are plain functions, not modules.

{2 Path parameters}

The router decodes a segment written [:name<Module.t>] with [Module.parse]
and prints it with [Module.to_string]:

{[
/* NoteId.re */
type t = int;
let parse = value =>
  switch (int_of_string_opt(value)) {
  | Some(id) when id >= 0 => Ok(id)
  | _ => Error("expected a note id")
  };
let to_string = string_of_int;
]}

Codecs receive percent-decoded segments. The router owns URL encoding. [int]
and [string] are built in. A failed parse renders the nearest error boundary
with [InvalidPathParameter].

{2 Search parameters}

Any scope declares search parameters as a typed record:

{[
~search={
  page: Router.Search.default(int, 1),
  filter: Router.Search.optional(Filter.t),
  tags: Router.Search.many(string),
  token: Router.Search.required(string),
}
]}

Child scopes see their ancestors' parameters as well as their own. Canonical
URLs omit values that equal their declared default. A required root search
parameter together with a root [~error] policy also requires [~invalidSearch].

{1 Generated application API}

Each route is a component and owns its typed helpers:

{[
<Router.Note id> children </Router.Note>

let destination = Router.Note.destination(~id, ())
let href = Router.Note.href(~id, ())

switch Router.useRoute() {
| Some(Router.Note({id})) => React.string(NoteId.to_string(id))
| _ => React.null
}
]}

[Router.useRoute] returns the active route as a generated variant with decoded
path parameters. It returns [None] when no route matched.

{2 Navigation}

[Router.useNavigation] returns the imperative navigate function and the
current navigation status:

{[
let (navigate, status) = Router.useNavigation();

let onSelect = id =>
  navigate(Router.Note.destination(~id, ())) |> ignore;

let isPending =
  switch (status) {
  | Router.Navigation.Loading(_) => true
  | Idle
  | Failed(_) => false
  };
]}

Navigating to a destination is a {e content} navigation. The client requests a
Flight model and the server answers with a full model, or with a partial patch
that reuses unchanged layouts. [~revalidate=true] forces a full response.
[~history] selects [Push], [Replace], or [Pop].

{2 Search updates}

[Router.useSearch] returns the decoded search record and its typed update
function:

{[
let ({Router.text: text}, updateSearch) = Router.useSearch();

let onChange = value => updateSearch(~text=Some(value), ()) |> ignore;
]}

Search updates are {e shallow}. They update the URL and client state
synchronously and never contact the server. When server-rendered content
depends on a search value, navigate to a destination carrying the new value
instead of calling [updateSearch].

[Router.useUpdateHash] commits hash-only changes without a request.

{1 Loaders}

A loader runs on the server before streaming. It receives the decoded path
parameters, the scope's search values, and ancestor loader results as labelled
arguments, and returns a [Router.Loader.result]:

{[
module NoteLoader = {
  let load = (~id, ~text as _, ()) =>
    DB.fetchNoteOption(id)
    |> Lwt.map(result =>
         switch (result) {
         | Ok(Some(note)) => Router.Loader.Data(note)
         | Ok(None) => Router.Loader.NotFound
         | Error(error) => Router.Loader.Error(error)
         }
       );
};
]}

Loaders execute parent-first and complete before response headers or RSC
rendering begin. The scope's components receive [Data] ([~note] in the
manifest above). [NotFound] and [Error] render the nearest boundary, and
[Redirect(destination)] escapes the whole branch.

A loader marks its scope and all descendants as non-reusable for navigation
patches. Keep loaders for fast validation, redirects, and route-level
not-found decisions; stream slow data from async server components inside the
route elements instead.

{1 Error handling}

The root [~error] policy maps application errors to statuses and renders them:

{[
module AppError = {
  type t = string;

  let status = _ => Router.Status.InternalServerError;

  [@react.component]
  let make = (~text as _, ~error as _) =>
    <p> "The requested note could not be loaded." </p>;
};
]}

[Router.Error.t] distinguishes [NotFound], [Application], invalid path or
search parameters, and [Internal] failures carrying only a diagnostic id.
Boundary components receive the scope inputs plus [~error]. The nearest
[~notFound] handles missing routes and loader [NotFound] results. Without one,
the nearest [~error] renders instead.

{1 Metadata and headers}

[~metadata] and [~headers] resolve per scope on every response, including
patches, and merge from the root down. Like loaders, they receive the decoded
path parameters, search values, and ancestor loader results, and return
[Lwt] promises:

{[
module NoteMetadata = {
  let make = (~id as _, ~text as _, ~note, ()) =>
    Lwt.return(Router.Metadata.make(~title=Note.title(note), ()));
};
]}

{1 Server integration}

The native generator emits [RouterRegistry.server]. A Dream application mounts
the generated router with one call:

{[
DreamRouter.routes(
  ~router=RouterRegistry.server,
  ~actionHandler,
  ~bootstrapModules=["/static/app.js"],
  ~document=children => <Document> children </Document>,
  (),
)
]}

The registry, matching engine, protocol version, response envelopes, and client
provider are implementation details of the generator and adapter.

{1 Libraries}

Melange applications depend on [server-reason-react.router]. Native
applications depend on [server-reason-react.router-native]. Route generation
uses [server-reason-react.router-gen].
